<p>The ability to mark items in GNU Emacs <a href="https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/emacs/Dired.html">Dired</a> allows for bulk operations on both files and directories. With marking, onerous tasks can be turned into a trivial ones.</p>
<p>While default Dired has <a href="http://yummymelon.com/devnull/bulk-search-replace-commands-for-files-and-buffers-in-emacs.html">many bulk operation commands</a>, not everything is covered. That is the motivation for <strong>mdired</strong>, an Elisp package which extends Dired with some <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Single_instruction,_multiple_data">SIMD-style</a> commands.</p>
<p>Using these commands fall into two steps:</p>
<ol>
<li>Mark the items of interest in Dired.</li>
<li>Run the <strong>mdired</strong> command.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>mdired</strong> supports the following four commands:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong><code>mdired-copy</code>:</strong> copy a single file to multiple directories.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong><code>mdired-do-command</code>:</strong> run an Elisp command or named keyboard macro on multiple files.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong><code>mdired-compile</code>:</strong> run <code>compile</code> on multiple directories.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong><code>mdired-byte-recompile</code>:</strong> byte-compile selected files or directories.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Refer to the <a href="https://kickingvegas.github.io/mdired/">mdired User Guide</a> for more details on usage.</p>
<h1>Closing Thoughts</h1>
<p>Bulk operation commands are chainsaws. Use them with caution. That said, they are great for improvised workflows:</p>
<ul>
<li>“Here’s this file that I want copied to a bunch of directories.”</li>
<li>“I want to do the same edit operation on this set of files. I’ve got a named keyboard macro ready to go on them.”</li>
<li>“I want to run the same make command on a bunch of directories.”</li>
</ul>
<p>As <strong>mdired</strong> is new, I’m open to <a href="https://github.com/kickingvegas/mdired/discussions">feedback</a> on it. Let me know what you think.</p>
